it's 1988 my mom is staring at a roomful  of people will decide the future of my  two older sisters my parents had been  told that their daughters would not be  allowed to enroll in the local  neighborhood elementary school instead  they would be shipped to a segregated  classroom for children with disabilities  two cities away my mom is furious she  argues that if the children of our  neighbors and friends attend the local  public school that her daughter's should  too the school board members speak at  length using lots of words about  logistics and capacity and cost to words  they never use are my sister's names  Sarah and Alison there were a lot of  meetings like this over the years of my  childhood Sarah Nelson both have severe  developmental disabilities and my  parents advocated tirelessly on their  behalf working with institutions ranging  from schools to health providers and  state agencies and I remember that there  was a clear difference between the  organizations that treated our family  like whole real complex human beings and  those that didn't most of us can  remember a time when we felt dehumanized  by an experience within a system maybe  you're thinking of your last interaction  with a customer service hotline or the  DMV yeah perhaps you're remembering a  tough navigation of the healthcare  system or an employer who made you feel  like a faceless number I am fascinated  by the question of what it means to  treat people like real people what it  looks like to offer the dignity risk  fact and compassion that we all crave in  our interactions and I'm especially  interested in how we as leaders can  create organizations that do just that  the treat people like real people I  believe it comes down to designing our  systems specifically and intentionally  for empathy so why is it important for  us to learn how to build empathy into  our organizations well I hope that all  of you have experienced the impact of  human kindness within a system for me I  think about the ER nurse last year who  long after midnight on one of the worst  nights of my life brought coffee and  snacks to the bedside where I sat with a  sick family member it turns out that  experiences of compassion like this one  have extremely real consequences for our  health research right here at Stanford  has shown that patients who receive  human kindness and empathy during their  treatment experience faster healing of  wounds reduced pain anxiety and blood  pressure and shorter hospital stays but  treating people like real people isn't  just important in the health sector  humans are evolutionarily wired with  what UC Berkeley researchers have coined  the compassion instinct we are driven to  empathize with the feelings of others  and to give and seek aid both engaging  in and receiving acts of compassion  results in physiological and  psychological benefits and quite simply  make for happier people so if we are  evolutionarily wired to be compassionate  toward one another why is it that so  many of us feel dehumanized by the  systems and organizations in our lives  well when I asked people what made them  feel human the most common  sponses were I feel like a real person  when people care about my background in  history when they listen to and know and  acknowledge my story people will tell  you I am NOT my disability I am NOT my  leukemia I am not my SAT score I am not  my depression I am not my immigration  status I am not my gender I am NOT this  singular interaction with you after all  we all contain multitudes I believe that  our organizations fail when they are not  designed and led to acknowledge these  multitudes empathy is what allows us in  our one-on-one interactions to treat  each other like real people to  understand that there is a full story  behind everyone we meet so what we need  for our organizations is a way to  systematize human empathy to do this I  suggest starting with three simple rules  listen contextualize and cultivate to  systematize empathy an organization has  to start by identifying its ears what  mechanisms does the organization use to  listen to its stakeholders  organizational listening can't just be  about a feedback box in the corner we  have to give stakeholders a valid place  at the table as respected partners an  example in practice right here in the  Bay Area juvenile justice systems are  working with the organization fresh  lifelines for youth to develop  mechanisms to listen to youth voices  these partnerships are helping to reduce  recidivism and educating the justice  system  on how to better serve its community the  second rule of building empathetic  organizations is operating with a  holistic appreciation for context when I  was working with healthcare  organizations in East Africa some of the  most effective models I saw were those  that have dressed issues intersecting  with patients health from nutrition and  sanitation to education and employment  these organizations approach their  patients as whole multifaceted people  and their medical outcomes were the  better for it  finally to cultivate empathy  organizations must unlock their most  powerful asset their own employees  research shows that stress burnout and  toxic company culture can squash our  compassion instinct but findings suggest  that companies that form a culture of  compassion not only boost employee  happiness and health they also improve  their bottom lines plus compassion is  proven to be contagious so if we want  our clients and customers to be treated  well we have to start by treating our  teammates like real whole people - back  in 1988 my mom told that school board  that if they didn't let her daughter's  into school she would sit with them on  the front lawn of Magnolia elementary  every single day until they did well  great job mom my parents ultimately won  their fight Seren Allison entered  Magnolia elementary school and  eventually Allison became our County's  first fully mainstreamed student with  disabilities to graduate from high  school my mom is very clear about one  important fact from our family's journey  it was not until there was organization  reform in districts and schools then my  sisters were truly treated like real  people so it's time for all of us to own  the fact that individual acts of  kindness are not enough it is time for  us to take organizational empathy out of  the realm of the random out of the realm  of above and beyond  and into the realm of the expected thank  you
 

